ers and  non-native speakers and very often  people of the same language group so  French speakers are surrounded by people  speaking with a French accent  Japanese speakers are on surrounded by  people's deep with the Japanese accent  when they're learning English so they  shouldn't listen to those people they  should listen to native speakers and  hopefully not just some boring stuff but  some something of interest and that's  what we see in Sweden where people learn  English primarily because of their  exposure to television movies and pop  culture in English to something that  they like and they find interesting and  therefore Swedes pronounce English very  well  the present generation whereas I would  imagine a previous generation two  generations ago that didn't have that  exposure would have more of a sweet  ushak simply this big English and  because this week is now growing up  aren't getting their English from their  classmates they're getting English from  movies and television this opens up so  it's very important to do a lot of  listening to authentic content one other  thing when we first start into a  language there's a lot of things we  don't hear so I often hear people say  well should I learn you know Spanish  from Spain or Spanish from Mexico so the  long as well it doesn't matter you can't  hear the difference if you're learning  you know if you know our learning polish  is probably four or five different  regional accents I won't be able to pick  them up it really doesn't matter to me  at some point though as I've got used to  the language and it's kind of fuzzy of a  broad sense of how it's pronounced I may  find certain people whose voice and  whose pronunciation and whose diction  then the subject matter interests me so  then I will really concentrate on  listening to those people and trying to  imitate them now in order to imitate you  have to notice and you can't make  assumptions about how things are  pronounced based on on your own language  so in Japanese I often mention the case  and Canadians go to Japan I was in  business there and I introduced  mr.kato and they say hello mr. Hinkle  because k80 always had potato my father  used to say the Bank of Nova Scotia  instead of Nova Scotia you know he would  say born forward and squiggle for  squirrel where is it word is grow so  he's trying to interpose or you know  impose the values of those letters from  check in his native language on to  English instead of listening and being  attentive to how those things are  pronounced in English I'm not a great  believer in the international phonetic  alphabet I think to me it's more a  matter of finding a voice an accent a  person an authentic native speaker that  you like subject matter is interesting  like Kim Jung ha the Korean podcast that  I've been listening to I love listening  to his voice I love his intonation I'm  gonna listen to him and I'm going to  model myself in Korean on kim yu-na  model yourself focus on listening and  don't worry about how you sound because  you are going to get better if you allow  yourself to be exposed to to the  language and the other thing is you have  to want to be one of them I think  there's a large sort of psychological  emotional aspect to pronouncing if you  hang back because you're you're  reluctant to do you don't want to be a  fool so you sort of hang back in your  own pronunciation or in my own case I'm  comfortable speaking Spanish when I go  to speak Portuguese  I find myself hanging on to my Spanish  rather than just totally throwing myself  into pronouncing the way the Portuguese  do whether it be in Portugal or Brazil  so you really have to take that plunge  just let yourself go and do a lot of  listening so I think if we do those  things and we don't need to worry we  don't have to do all our listening you  know I just so much listening reading  when I learn a language not all of the  listing that I do is to people whom I  want to be like some of I miss some of  that I listen to for the content for the  language  to require vocabulary but then there are  some of it where the voice is so nice  the pronunciation intonation is so nice  that's where I really focus on that's  what I imitate insofar as my  pronunciations concern so I hope that's  helpful again I also caution that we  aren't going to sound like a native  speaker that in most cases we can aspire  to what we shouldn't be disappointed if  we don't reach that level and I'm far  more impressed by people who speak with  an accent but speak well and have you  know good use of vocabulary than I am  with people who seem to have very you  know native or almost native like  pronunciation but who can't really you  know express themselves eloquently or  precisely in the language so there you  have it just to follow up on that  previous video about accents is just  that I throw in a one on how to achieve  an a native light or close to native  light accent or what we might call  pleasing pronunciation in our language  thank you for listening
 

